 "transcript": {
  "answer": "**Short answer:** Perth electricity tariffs aren't rising unusually fast. Your bill may still feel big compared with a few months ago, and the reason is probably the end of government credits.\n\n**ABS CPI for electricity, August 2026**\n- **Perth:** prices are up **2.4%** on a year earlier. That is below Perth's overall inflation of **4.3%**.\n- **Australia (eight-capital average):** electricity is up **13.2%** on a year earlier. It was 22.4% in June 2026 and 37% in February 2026.\n- Perth's electricity index was 100 in July 2025 and is 102.4 in July and August 2026. That matches a small annual tariff increase of about 2.4–2.5%.\n\n**Why it may feel worse than that**\n- Perth's monthly electricity figures swing a lot. The year-on-year change ran from **-50%** to **+289%** between 2024 and 2026.\n- The index fell as low as 25.7 in late 2024 and early 2025, and to 69–79 in October to December 2025.\n- I'm inferring the cause, since the data doesn't name it: these dips look like the State and Federal bill credits, which the CPI counts as lower prices.\n- Once the credits stopped, the index went back to about 100 by February 2026. If your bills were cushioned by credits before, your current bill looks much larger. It is close to the full tariff, not a sudden price jump.\n\n**Other possible causes**\n- Winter heating and cooling use.\n- A higher-usage quarter.\n- A change in your plan or tariff.\n\nThis is a city-wide index, so it can't show your own bill. To check, compare your usage in kWh with the same period last year, and look for credits on your old bills that aren't on the new one.",
